The Security Council,

Guided by the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,

Recalling its resolutions 1645 (2005), 2282 (2016), 2413 (2018), and 2558 (2020), as well as relevant General Assembly resolutions, including resolutions 60/180, 70/262, 75/201, and 78/257, and reaffirming the importance of an integrated, strategic, and coherent approach across the United Nations system to peacebuilding and sustaining peace,

Reaffirming that “sustaining peace” should be broadly understood as a goal and a process encompassing activities aimed at preventing the outbreak, escalation, continuation, and recurrence of conflict, addressing root causes, assisting parties to conflict to end hostilities, ensuring national reconciliation, and moving towards recovery, reconstruction, and development,

Emphasizing that sustaining peace is a shared responsibility of Governments and all other national stakeholders, and underlining that primary responsibility lies with national Governments, with full respect for national ownership and sovereignty,

Recognizing the vital advisory role of the Peacebuilding Commission in supporting the Security Council, upon its request, in addressing the peacebuilding and sustaining peace dimensions of situations on its agenda, particularly during the drawdown, withdrawal, and transition of United Nations peacekeeping operations and special political missions,

Recalling the commitments made in the Pact for the Future to strengthen the United Nations peacebuilding architecture, enhance conflict prevention, and ensure more predictable, adequate, and sustained financing for peacebuilding,

Recognizing the indispensable contribution of women and youth to peacebuilding and conflict resolution, and reaffirming the full, equal, and meaningful participation of women and the inclusion of youth in all peace and security processes,

Welcoming the ongoing 2025 comprehensive review of the United Nations peacebuilding architecture, and commending the work of the Peacebuilding Commission, the Peacebuilding Support Office, and the Peacebuilding Fund,

1. Welcomes the progress achieved in the implementation of the resolutions on peacebuilding and sustaining peace, and encourages Member States, the Peacebuilding Commission, and the United Nations system to continue intensifying their efforts;

2. Decides to make greater and more systematic use of the advisory role of the Peacebuilding Commission, including through seeking its timely, specific, and actionable advice during the formation, review, and transition of peacekeeping and special political mission mandates, in accordance with the Charter of the United Nations;

3. Encourages the Peacebuilding Commission to continue bridging the work of the Security Council, the General Assembly, and the Economic and Social Council, within their respective mandates, fostering coherence across the peace, development, and humanitarian nexus;

4. Underlines the necessity of adequate, predictable, and sustained financing for peacebuilding, acknowledges the adoption of General Assembly resolution 78/257 on the financing of the Peacebuilding Fund, and encourages continued voluntary contributions alongside established funding mechanisms to meet growing needs;

5. Reaffirms the importance of national ownership and leadership in peacebuilding strategies, and stresses the need to support national capacities, institutions, the rule of law, and good governance to prevent relapses into conflict;

6. Urges the full, equal, and meaningful participation of women and the meaningful inclusion of youth in all stages of peacebuilding, national dialogue, and post-conflict recovery, and calls upon United Nations entities to integrate gender and youth perspectives across all peacebuilding planning and implementation;

7. Takes note of the ongoing 2025 review of the peacebuilding architecture, and requests the Secretary-General to submit to the General Assembly and the Security Council a comprehensive report on the implementation of the recommendations emanating from the 2025 review by the end of the eighty-first session;

8. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

The Security Council,

Guided by the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations, including the peaceful settlement of disputes,

Recalling its resolutions 1645 (2005), 2282 (2016), 2413 (2018), and 2558 (2020), as well as relevant General Assembly resolutions, including resolutions 60/180, 70/262, 75/201, and 78/257, and reaffirming the importance of an integrated, strategic, and coherent approach across the United Nations system to peacebuilding and sustaining peace,

Reaffirming that “sustaining peace” should be broadly understood as a goal and a process encompassing activities aimed at preventing the outbreak, escalation, continuation, and recurrence of conflict, addressing root causes, assisting parties to conflict to end hostilities, ensuring national reconciliation, and moving towards recovery, reconstruction, and development,

Emphasizing that sustaining peace is a shared responsibility of Governments and all other national stakeholders, and underlining that primary responsibility lies with national Governments, with full respect for national ownership and sovereignty,

Recognizing the vital advisory role of the Peacebuilding Commission in supporting the Security Council, upon its request and in consultation with the countries concerned, in addressing the peacebuilding and sustaining peace dimensions of situations on its agenda, particularly during the drawdown, withdrawal, and transition of United Nations peacekeeping operations and special political missions,

Recognizing the critical role of regional and subregional organizations, in particular the African Union, in peacebuilding and sustaining peace, and highlighting the importance of deepening partnerships between the United Nations, the Peacebuilding Commission, and regional arrangements in line with Chapter VIII of the Charter, including through the implementation of the African Union Post-Conflict Reconstruction and Development policy,

Recognizing that the adverse impacts of climate change, ecological degradation, and food and water insecurity, combined with socioeconomic disparities, can exacerbate existing vulnerabilities and conflict drivers, and underscoring the importance of addressing root causes of conflict in comprehensive peacebuilding strategies,

Recognizing the indispensable contribution of women and youth to peacebuilding and conflict resolution, and reaffirming the full, equal, and meaningful participation of women and the inclusion of youth in all peace and security processes,

Welcoming the ongoing 2025 comprehensive review of the United Nations peacebuilding architecture, and commending the work of the Peacebuilding Commission, the Peacebuilding Support Office, and the Peacebuilding Fund,

1. Welcomes the progress achieved in the implementation of the resolutions on peacebuilding and sustaining peace, and encourages Member States, the Peacebuilding Commission, and the United Nations system to continue intensifying their efforts;

2. Expresses its intention to make more systematic and regular use of the advisory role of the Peacebuilding Commission, in close consultation with host States and troop- and police-contributing countries as appropriate, including through seeking its timely, specific, and actionable advice during the formation, review, and transition of peacekeeping and special political mission mandates, in accordance with the Charter of the United Nations;

3. Encourages the Peacebuilding Commission to continue bridging the work of the Security Council, the General Assembly, and the Economic and Social Council, within their respective mandates and respecting the primary responsibility of the Security Council for the maintenance of international peace and security, fostering coherence across the peace, development, and humanitarian nexus, and to further strengthen its collaboration with regional and subregional organizations, including the African Union Peace and Security Council, as well as international financial institutions to mobilize resources and expertise for sustained peacebuilding efforts;

4. Underlines the necessity of adequate, predictable, and sustained financing for peacebuilding, acknowledges the adoption of General Assembly resolution 78/257 on the financing of the Peacebuilding Fund, emphasizes the importance of predictable and sustainable financing for African-led peacebuilding and conflict prevention initiatives, and encourages continued voluntary contributions alongside established funding mechanisms to meet growing needs, while noting diverse perspectives on financing modalities;

5. Reaffirms the importance of national ownership and leadership in peacebuilding strategies, with full respect for national sovereignty, political independence, and the consent of the host State, stresses that peacebuilding must align with national priorities and address the root causes of conflict, including through socioeconomic development and poverty eradication, and emphasizes the need to support national capacities, institutions, the rule of law, the promotion and protection of human rights and fundamental freedoms, good governance, and the meaningful engagement of civil society organizations and local communities to prevent relapses into conflict, while calling for enhanced international support for post-conflict reconstruction and institution-building;

6. Urges the full, equal, and meaningful participation of women and the meaningful inclusion of youth and civil society organizations in all stages of peacebuilding, national dialogue, and post-conflict recovery, reaffirms the need to create a safe and enabling environment for women peacebuilders and human rights defenders, protecting them from violence and reprisals, and calls upon United Nations entities to integrate gender and youth perspectives across all peacebuilding planning and implementation;

7. Takes note of the ongoing 2025 review of the peacebuilding architecture, and requests the Secretary-General to submit to the General Assembly and the Security Council a comprehensive report on the implementation of the recommendations emanating from the 2025 review by the end of the eighty-first session;

8. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

